.\" dashlock-check(8): the dashlock policy checker
.\" BSD-3-Clause, see the dashlock documentation
.Dd October 6, 2026
.Dt DASHLOCK-CHECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dashlock-check
.Nd check a dashlock policy without confining anything
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl k | Fl d
.Op Fl l
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl n Ar narrow
.Op Ar user
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reports whether a
.Xr dash 1
confinement policy is well-formed and whether it would apply, so that an
administrator can check a policy before it reaches a session instead of
learning of a mistake from a broken login.
It is built from the same source as the confining shell and runs the
shell's own user-key resolution, policy lookup, trust checks on the policy
path, parser, and pre-kernel checks of the enforcement backend, in the
order the shell runs them.
It stops before enforcement: no ruleset is created, no
.Xr unveil 2
or
.Xr pledge 2
call is made, and the invoking process is never confined.
.Pp
A refusal is the shell's own: the same message on standard error and the
same exit status 78 that a session would produce for that policy.
Running
.Nm
therefore reproduces a session refusal and yields the session's report.
The trust checks on the policy path run as the invoking identity, so a run
by root validates what root would receive and a run by an unprivileged
account validates what that account would receive; the report states the
identity it was made for.
.Pp
Without
.Ar user ,
the policy of the invoking account is checked.
With
.Ar user ,
the policy that account would receive is checked, through the same lookup
order.
A
.Ar user
that is not a local account but is a usable policy key is accepted
verbatim, so that a policy file can be checked before the account exists;
the report says so.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k
Kernel mode.
In addition to the structural checks, query the running Landlock ABI and
report the derived required ABI, the explicit floor, the ceiling in force,
and the kernel value, then apply the same ABI checks the session applies.
The judgment holds for this kernel only; the report says so.
On the unveil backend there is no version gate and the mode reports that.
.It Fl d
Dump mode.
Print the normalized policy, one fact per line: the backend, the ABI floor
and ceiling, the handled categories, the scope set, the pledge set, every
path rule and port rule with its rights in a fixed order, and the derived
required ABI.
The output is deterministic, so two dumps can be compared with
.Xr diff 1
and a dump can be stored next to a policy as its reviewed meaning.
With
.Fl n ,
the base policy and the narrowing policy are printed as two separate
blocks; no intersection is computed, because only the kernel intersects
layers.
.It Fl l
Emit advisory lints on standard error.
Lints name omissions that read like permission bugs in a session, such as
a library directory without read and execute, a loader cache that cannot
be read, terminal devices without
.Cm ioctl-dev ,
a handled category without rules, or a home directory no rule covers.
They are recommendations and never change the exit status.
.It Fl q
Quiet: print no success line; findings stay one per line.
.It Fl n Ar narrow
Also check the narrowing policy
.Ar narrow ,
exactly as a session started with
.Fl -narrow
would: on the Landlock backend it is located, trust-checked, parsed, and
cross-checked after the base policy; on the unveil backend the request is
refused before any policy file is read.
.It Fl V
Print the backend this binary enforces with and the configured policy
directories, then exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The policy would apply.
.It 78
The policy would not apply; the reason is on standard error, worded as the
session would word it.
.It 2
Usage error.
This is distinct from a refusal, so that a wrong command line is never
mistaken for a policy problem.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check the policy the account
.Ql agent
would receive, with lints:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dashlock-check -l agent
.Ed
.Pp
Compare the meaning of a policy before and after an edit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dashlock-check -d agent > before
# vi /etc/dashlock/users/agent
# dashlock-check -d agent | diff before -
.Ed
.Pp
Gate a policy repository in continuous integration, one finding per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dashlock-check -q -l template-account; echo $?
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dash 1 ,
.Xr landlock 7 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2
.Pp
The DASHLOCK section of
.Xr dash 1
describes the policy format and the refusal messages.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
answers whether a policy is well-formed and applicable.
It cannot show that a session under the policy can log in and work; that
question belongs to a disposable session under the policy, probing the
operations it is expected to allow and deny.
